<h1><%= article.title %></h1>
<%- include('../partials/byline') %>
<p class="muted"><%= article.deck %></p>

<p>The forearm is where tattoos went public. It is the canvas you show the room every time you reach for a door handle, shake a hand or roll a sleeve — and the canvas you see yourself, hundreds of times a day, for the rest of your life. That double visibility makes the forearm the most consequential placement most people ever choose, and it deserves more than the usual shrug of "it doesn't hurt much, go big." This guide works the whole brief: the forearm's two different faces and who they are for, the honest pain report, how designs distort on a limb that twists, sizing that survives, the sun-and-work problem nobody mentions at the consultation, and how forearm pieces age when they are lived in rather than photographed once.</p>

<h2>Two forearms in one: the face that talks to you, and the one that talks to the room</h2>
<p>Every forearm design has an orientation decision hiding inside it, and it is the first real choice. The <strong>inner forearm</strong> — the pale, softer underside — is the personal face. Work placed there reads to you, upright when your arm hangs and you glance down, and it shows the room only when you turn your wrist. It is also the more protected skin: less sun, less friction, slower wear. The <strong>outer forearm</strong> — the top, from wrist toward elbow — is the public face. It reads to everyone else, photographs the way it will be seen, and takes the weather of a life: sun through windshields, sleeves, desk edges, gym equipment. Neither is the correct answer; they are different uses. Script and fine, private work often belongs inside. Bold statements, traditional pieces and anything meant to be read across a room belong outside. And orientation within the face matters too: a design should read upright to its intended audience — which is why the stencil moment, walked through in our <a href="/guides/stencil-day">stencil-day guide</a>, includes the unglamorous step of standing up, arms hanging naturally, and checking the mirror before a single line is inked.</p>

<h2>The pain report, honestly</h2>
<p>The forearm is one of the kinder placements, and the reputation is earned. The outer forearm is mostly muscle and padding over bone with a moderate nerve supply: most people rate it a two or three out of ten, a scratchy annoyance that fades into the background of a session. The inner forearm runs a little livelier — the skin is thinner, the tendons and veins sit closer to the surface — call it a three to four, with two hot corners: the <strong>inner wrist</strong>, where the skin creases and nerves cluster, and the <strong>inner elbow ditch</strong>, which nearly everyone agrees is the forearm's one genuinely spicy address. Sessions also change the math: a forearm piece is usually a single sitting of one to three hours, short enough that endurance rarely becomes the issue it is on ribs or backs. If pain is your deciding factor between the forearm and somewhere braver, the forearm is the friendly choice — but read our placement-and-pain article before you let comfort pick the canvas, because the right question is not "where does it hurt least" but "where will I still love it at fifty."</p>

<h2>Twist: the forearm is not a flat board</h2>
<p>Here is the thing drawings on paper do not tell you: your forearm rotates. The radius bone rolls over the ulna every time you turn a key, and the skin of the forearm shears with it — a design that sits perfectly when your palm faces down can lean several degrees when your palm faces up. Good artists draw for this. Vertical compositions — a dagger, a stem of flowers, a column of script — are stenciled with the arm in its natural hang and checked through the rotation, and designs with strong symmetry or straight edges (geometric work, architecture, text) get extra scrutiny because the twist shows in them first. Wrap-around designs live with the twist rather than fighting it: water, smoke, vines, snakes and other flowing subjects turn the rotation into movement, which is why the gallery's animal and nature work wraps forearms so naturally. The practical takeaway for choosing: if your design has a hard straight line in it, expect your artist to spend stencil time on it, and be suspicious of any artist who doesn't.</p>

<h2>Sizing for a limb that tapers</h2>
<p>The forearm is a truncated cone, wider at the elbow than the wrist, and good compositions respect the taper — slightly narrower toward the wrist, breathing room at both ends rather than ink running edge to edge. Working sizes: a single emblem or subject usually runs four to seven inches long on the outer forearm; inner-forearm pieces run a touch smaller; a full forearm wrap is a panel of roughly nine to eleven inches around depending on the arm. The detail law applies here as everywhere — fine lines and small faces want their minimums — but the forearm has a second sizing consideration most placements don't: <strong>it is the first piece of a common future</strong>. A forearm tattoo today is often next year's half-sleeve. If there is any chance you will build upward, tell your artist now; leaving the elbow end of the composition open, and choosing background elements that can extend, is the difference between a sleeve that was planned and one that was collided with. Browse the <a href="/gallery?cat=<%= encodeURIComponent(article.category) %>">Animals &amp; Wildlife shelf</a> with that lens and you will see which compositions are drawn as chapters and which are drawn as whole books.</p>

<h2>Sun, sleeves and the working arm</h2>
<p>Forearms live outdoors. The left forearm of anyone who drives takes a famous stripe of sun through the window; both forearms take sleeves pushed up, watches, fitness bands, desk edges and everything a working day rubs against skin. Two consequences follow. First, color discipline: the forearm spends pigments faster than almost any covered placement, so saturated color work there wants a sunscreen habit once healed — the long game is covered in our <a href="/guides/aftercare">aftercare guide</a> — and black-and-grey or bold traditional work shrugs sun off more gracefully. Second, the professional question, which has genuinely changed in a decade: most workplaces now ignore a forearm tattoo entirely, but not all, and not everywhere — client-facing roles, some uniforms, some countries' business cultures still read visible ink as a statement you may not intend to make at work. The honest test is the wrist line: a design that starts two inches above the wrist disappears under a cuff; one that runs to the hand does not. Decide where your cuff line is before the stencil, not after the promotion interview.</p>

<h2>How forearm tattoos age</h2>
<p>Well, as placements go — with one asterisk. The skin is stable, the area doesn't stretch and shrink with weight the way stomachs and upper arms can, and the muscle underneath holds its shape for decades. Traditional and blackwork pieces on forearms are famous for looking right at twenty years. The asterisk is exposure: sun fade arrives earlier here than anywhere covered, fine lines soften sooner on the outer face than the inner, and the wrist crease will work on anything inked across it. All of which is manageable — placement inside the forearm for delicate work, honest sizing, sunscreen as a habit — and none of which should scare you off the most communicative canvas you own. The <a href="/styles/fine-line">fine line style guide</a> has the deeper aging notes if delicate work is your direction.</p>

<h2>Choosing a forearm design</h2>
<p>Work the brief in order: face first (inner for you, outer for the room), orientation upright to its audience, a composition that either respects the twist or uses it, sized to the taper with the elbow end's future in mind, and honest about sun. Animals, daggers, florals, script and instruments all live well here — which is why forearms anchor so many of the gallery's <a href="/gallery?cat=<%= encodeURIComponent(article.category) %>">animal designs</a>: a wolf or an owl drawn vertically owns this limb. Every premade design arrives with the full-color art and the clean linework, and if the piece you want needs its proportions tuned to your arm — lengthened, narrowed, flipped to read on the other forearm — a custom variation redraws it to your measurements. Your forearm is the page the world reads first. Write something on it you are still proud to show at sixty.</p>

